47
00:04:51,906 --> 00:05:02,809
Tonight we'll be diving into the communities of Brookline, Cambridge, Medford, Somerville, and Watertown, and what the MBTA's Bus Network Redesign proposal can mean for these municipalities.

48
00:05:04,529 --> 00:05:14,074
For tonight's meeting, we will have a presentation that I will co-lead with my colleague, Melissa Dulay, who's a senior director of service planning for the MBTA.

49
00:05:16,516 --> 00:05:29,443
After we review the proposal and the presentation, we'll break into small group discussions where you'll have an opportunity to talk with MBTA project team staff, review the proposals closely and provide more feedback.

50
00:05:30,683 --> 00:05:37,754
We'll then reconvene as a larger group, where you'll have an opportunity for questions and answers if you didn't get any of your questions answered in the smaller groups.

51
00:05:39,496 --> 00:05:44,604
Before we get started, I want to introduce the MBTA General Manager Steve Poptak to give some opening remarks.

52
00:05:45,025 --> 00:05:45,586
General Manager.

53
00:05:46,798 --> 00:05:47,779
Thanks so much, Andrew.

54
00:05:48,340 --> 00:05:58,611
I'm here live from Mattapan station, one of our really important nodes with both BUS and obviously the Mattapan trolley.

55
00:05:58,891 --> 00:06:01,574
And I want to thank everyone who joined us today.

56
00:06:01,594 --> 00:06:08,542
I want to welcome everyone who's joining us for this important conversation about the future of our BUS network.

57
00:06:09,322 --> 00:06:19,004
As you know, as everyone on this call knows, transit is really essential to this region's economy and our bus system serves our most transit dependent populations.

58
00:06:19,764 --> 00:06:26,685
And while this bus system has grown over the years, this area has experienced such dynamic change.

59
00:06:26,805 --> 00:06:29,145
We need a bus service that changes with it.

60
00:06:30,006 --> 00:06:34,066
And we're really excited to share this draft network map with you.

61
00:06:34,586 --> 00:06:38,627
We really view this process as a once in a generation opportunity to make

62
00:06:39,283 --> 00:06:45,246
bold improvements to the MBTA's bus network and to do it for the people who depend on us most.

63
00:06:46,087 --> 00:06:56,032
So this is not only a redesign of the bus system, but to be clear, it's also increasing the amount of bus service that we are delivering.

64
00:06:56,193 --> 00:07:01,656
The plan over the next five years is to increase bus service by 25% and that includes a 70% increase on weekends.

65
00:07:01,676 --> 00:07:02,116
It also means

66
00:07:06,519 --> 00:07:10,120
doubling the amount of routes that have high frequency service.

67
00:07:10,220 --> 00:07:16,321
And that's that's what we define as a bus stopping every 15 minutes or less throughout the service day.

68
00:07:16,802 --> 00:07:19,122
Now these changes can sometimes be difficult.

69
00:07:19,142 --> 00:07:20,983
There can sometimes be tradeoffs.

70
00:07:21,123 --> 00:07:26,104
But I believe the benefits to improving our bus system are really clear.

71
00:07:26,785 --> 00:07:29,468
So I thank you for your participation tonight.

72
00:07:29,748 --> 00:07:33,432
We're really excited to hear from all of you, particularly our bus riders.

73
00:07:34,433 --> 00:07:39,097
And I just want to reemphasize this draft network map is just that.

74
00:07:39,198 --> 00:07:39,838
It's a draft.

75
00:07:40,179 --> 00:07:46,545
We're really looking to make changes and hear your feedback and make this system as good as it can possibly be for our riders.

76
00:07:46,905 --> 00:07:48,386
So again, thank you for joining.

77
00:07:48,547 --> 00:07:50,789
Thank you for engaging in this process.

78
00:07:51,089 --> 00:07:55,112
And thank you for caring about the bus system here and our bus riders.

79
00:07:55,573 --> 00:08:02,018
So with that, I will hand it back to Andrew and turn off my mic here.

80
00:08:02,038 --> 00:08:06,822
I know it's a little noisy behind me, but I appreciate the opportunity to say hello to you all.

81
00:08:09,264 --> 00:08:12,126
Thank you, General Manager, for those exciting live remarks.

82
00:08:13,437 --> 00:08:16,859
I can't think of a better place to do that than live at our bus stop.

83
00:08:18,820 --> 00:08:23,263
Before we get started, we want to do a quick poll just to get a sense of who's in the room.

84
00:08:24,384 --> 00:08:26,725
Jason, could you activate the poll, please?

85
00:08:29,747 --> 00:08:31,448
So a poll should be popping up on your screen.

86
00:08:31,948 --> 00:08:37,812
We're asking folks, how regularly did you ride the bus and whether you've ridden the bus in the last month?

87
00:08:37,832 --> 00:08:42,695
So we'll give folks a few seconds to fill that out.

88
00:09:06,523 --> 00:09:08,483
Awesome, it looks like most folks have filled it out.

89
00:09:09,564 --> 00:09:10,844
So let's wrap up the poll.

90
00:09:11,064 --> 00:09:17,685
And it looks like, according to this, about 84% of you are regular bus riders, which is great.

91
00:09:18,265 --> 00:09:21,206
Really glad to see there's so many bus riders in the room tonight.

92
00:09:22,346 --> 00:09:28,727
And for the 16% of you who are maybe not regular bus riders, we hope that you are future riders under this new system.

93
00:09:30,008 --> 00:09:31,768
So with that, let's get started with the presentation.

94
00:09:33,652 --> 00:09:35,733
So what is bus network redesign?

95
00:09:36,494 --> 00:09:40,356
We know that the MBTA service area has changed a lot in the last few decades.

96
00:09:41,136 --> 00:09:50,721
New destinations and districts have emerged, areas like the Kendall Square area, Longwood Medical Area, Seaport District, Assembly Square.

97
00:09:51,622 --> 00:09:55,884
And we know that our bus system hasn't always met the needs of these growing districts.

98
00:09:56,885 --> 00:10:02,648
We also know that travel patterns have changed a lot in the last few years, especially in the wake of the COVID-19 pandemic.

99
00:10:03,504 --> 00:10:10,271
And we want to make sure that our bus system really meets the needs of folks across the region, but especially those who need the service the most.

100
00:10:11,292 --> 00:10:21,323
So bus network redesign is just that, our effort to holistically reimagine the MBTA bus network and making sure that we're meeting the needs of current and future riders over the next few years.

101
00:10:25,354 --> 00:10:35,236
The bus network redesign is just one in a suite of initiatives that the MBTA is undertaking to really improve bus service and transform it for bus riders over the next few years.

102
00:10:36,116 --> 00:10:46,818
In addition to rethinking our bus routes, we are undertaking a major fleet and facilities program where we're looking to renovate and expand our bus garages and maintenance facilities.

103
00:10:48,058 --> 00:10:54,019
As part of that, we're also looking to transition to an electric fleet, making sure that we have the buses that we need to run more service.

104
00:10:55,786 --> 00:11:02,910
Additionally, we're working closely with cities and towns and other roadway owners to implement more bus priority on congested roadways.

105
00:11:03,891 --> 00:11:16,598
We know that a lot of our buses get stuck in traffic, and so we want to make sure that we're working with municipalities to put in bus lanes, traffic signal upgrades called transit signal priority, and other design upgrades.

106
00:11:17,598 --> 00:11:30,724
We've worked closely with many of the municipalities, especially the ones that are included in this meeting tonight, to do just that, to add more bus lanes, to make sure that we're getting the reliable service that we're looking for.

107
00:11:31,804 --> 00:11:45,870
Additionally, we're looking to improve the accessibility of bus stops, add more bus shelters, and there's another initiative underway to improve our fare collection system through fare transformation, where we're looking to move towards a system that would allow for all-door boarding.

108
00:11:50,613 --> 00:11:53,895
So with this proposed network, we're really trying to achieve five goals.

109
00:11:54,556 --> 00:12:14,749
First and foremost, we're trying to improve, make sure that our bus system is meeting the needs of those who need it the most, and making sure that we're concentrating those needs in areas where there are high rates of low income populations, people of color, seniors, people with disabilities, and people who live in households without access to a car.

110
00:12:15,549 --> 00:12:19,312
So I'll make sure that we're delivering the biggest benefits for these populations.

111
00:12:20,650 --> 00:12:23,252
Secondly, we're trying to provide a lot more frequent service.

112
00:12:24,152 --> 00:12:28,815
Time and time again, we hear from our riders, they want to see more frequent services process system.

113
00:12:29,355 --> 00:12:32,577
So like the general manager said, we're looking to double those in the coming years.

114
00:12:34,353 --> 00:12:36,554
We're also trying to provide a lot more all-day service.

115
00:12:37,594 --> 00:12:41,315
Traditionally, we've been very focused on trying to get folks to work at 9 a.m.

116
00:12:41,335 --> 00:12:43,595
and getting them home from work at 5 p.m.

117
00:12:44,075 --> 00:12:47,556
But in reality, we know that folks have a lot of different trip needs.

118
00:12:48,276 --> 00:12:50,737
Folks want to see more midday service, so they get to appointments.

119
00:12:51,457 --> 00:12:56,178
They want to have service later in the evening, so they can go see friends or get home from work later.

120
00:12:56,918 --> 00:12:59,584
We're also trying to provide a lot more service on the weekends.

121
00:13:00,806 --> 00:13:06,158
Many parts of our system don't have Sunday service, so we're trying to provide more seven-day service system-wide.

122
00:13:07,675 --> 00:13:10,417
Fourth, we're trying to provide new connections to more places.

123
00:13:11,117 --> 00:13:17,481
So I mentioned some of those growing areas earlier, the Seaport District, Assembly Square, Longwood Medical Area.

124
00:13:18,341 --> 00:13:28,007
We know that folks want to get to these areas, but there are also a lot of other areas, shopping plazas, community centers, other housing complexes that folks need to get to.

125
00:13:28,027 --> 00:13:31,029
So we're trying to provide more connections to places like that.

126
00:13:32,650 --> 00:13:36,634
Fifth, and finally, we're trying to provide a system that's simpler and easier to use.

127
00:13:37,255 --> 00:13:45,684
A lot of our routes have variations where they might have one type of trip at different times of the day, or they might not have service on the weekends.

128
00:13:45,704 --> 00:13:49,688
So we're trying to provide more consistent service seven days a week, 20 hours a day.

129
00:13:54,394 --> 00:14:00,398
So with this proposal, we are committed to a 25% increase in bus service hours over the next five years.

130
00:14:01,239 --> 00:14:06,342
This is really an unprecedented commitment to expand bus service, and we're really excited about this.

131
00:14:06,482 --> 00:14:12,226
We think this will have a major impact for how people travel in and around the Boston area in the years to come.

132
00:14:13,347 --> 00:14:15,848
Included in this 25% increase is a 70% increase in weekend service alone.

133
00:14:18,539 --> 00:14:22,961
And all this increase in service really allows us to serve many, many more riders.

134
00:14:23,402 --> 00:14:28,504
We estimated about 300,000 new residents would be within walking distance.

135
00:14:28,844 --> 00:14:32,746
That's within a quarter mile or so of a new high frequency service.

136
00:14:33,187 --> 00:14:37,489
That's a bus that comes every 15 minutes or better, seven days a week, 20 hours a day.

137
00:14:39,248 --> 00:14:49,773
In the Longwood Medical Area alone, we estimate that about 200,000 more residents will be within walking distance of a new high-frequency service that connects directly to the Longwood Medical Area.

138
00:14:50,373 --> 00:14:57,797
It also allows us to provide a lot more service to our equity populations, making sure that our bus system is providing the public good that it's intended to.

139
00:15:02,960 --> 00:15:07,441
As you mentioned earlier, we are proposing a doubling of high-frequency service system-wide.

140
00:15:08,381 --> 00:15:13,043
Today, we have about 15 routes that would be considered high-frequency service.

141
00:15:13,483 --> 00:15:15,243
These are often called our key bus routes.

142
00:15:15,583 --> 00:15:20,205
They include buses like the number 1, the 77, 71, the 66.

143
00:15:21,725 --> 00:15:25,066
These are some of our most frequent and most popular routes system-wide.

144
00:15:25,546 --> 00:15:27,847
And we know that folks want to see a lot more services like those.

145
00:15:28,907 --> 00:15:36,917
So in our new system, we are proposing going from 15 corridors today to 30 corridors that would be considered high frequency in the future.

146
00:15:36,937 --> 00:15:44,186
This means that half of the service that we would be providing would be high frequency service, which we're very excited about.

147
00:15:45,253 --> 00:15:51,234
This also allows us to add some municipalities to the high-frequency map that weren't historically included in that map.

148
00:15:51,914 --> 00:15:57,736
Those include places like Everett, Lynn, Medford, Somerville, and many parts of Austin.

149
00:15:58,576 --> 00:16:07,618
We're also looking to provide more crosstown connections to connect folks to and from these high-frequency services, and also make sure they can get to and from rapid transit more easily.

150
00:16:12,007 --> 00:16:17,918
So with that, I will pass it off to my colleague, Melissa Joulet, who's the Senior Director of Service Planning for the MBTA.

151
00:16:17,938 --> 00:16:18,539
Melissa.

152
00:16:20,462 --> 00:16:21,985
Thanks for that introduction, Andrew.

153
00:16:24,167 --> 00:16:30,352
I'll start here with the map and describe the changes that we have coming for the north half of Brookline.

154
00:16:30,732 --> 00:16:34,856
I apologize, these are excerpted from some other materials that we've prepared.

155
00:16:35,476 --> 00:16:43,842
So this does include Austin and Brighton, but those were covered in our Boston area meeting, so I'll only be addressing the Brookline North changes.

156
00:16:45,583 --> 00:16:54,346
Another bookkeeping thing here, you'll see that we have identified some bus routes here with the nomenclature.

157
00:16:55,086 --> 00:16:58,328
The working name, we have the letter T and a number.

158
00:16:58,688 --> 00:17:02,589
That's what we're using to indicate any of the high frequency bus services.

159
00:17:03,009 --> 00:17:08,551
Those are the routes that we were talking about that are proposed to arrive every 15 minutes or better, seven days a week.

160
00:17:09,011 --> 00:17:10,032
for 20 hours a day.

161
00:17:10,672 --> 00:17:20,436
We were trying to come up with a way to say these are different from other routes in that you many times wouldn't even need a schedule to be able to use them.

162
00:17:20,476 --> 00:17:25,538
You can sort of show up with the expectation that within a reasonable amount of time, a bus would be along.

163
00:17:26,158 --> 00:17:33,981
Though the nomenclature of the T66 or the T70, that might be updated.

164
00:17:34,061 --> 00:17:35,462
So that's kind of our working name.

165
00:17:35,926 --> 00:17:44,514
For now, for reference, we were riffing off of the, you know, taking the MBTA because we want to make sure that bus is considered part of the network.

166
00:17:44,974 --> 00:17:50,820
Technically, these are all the frequent routes, but F routes sounded so, had sort of a negative connotation.

167
00:17:51,900 --> 00:18:04,346
So we're very excited to show that Brookline North, specifically the area getting on the approach to Longwood Medical Area, has many new high-frequency connections that are added to the map.

168
00:18:05,266 --> 00:18:15,050
There are already some existing connections, so you can see a route like the T57, which is pretty much identical to today's 57 bus.

169
00:18:15,090 --> 00:18:18,432
So actually, even for existing key bus routes like the 57,

170
00:18:19,372 --> 00:18:27,158
We are proposing to improve the off-peak frequency to be every 15 minutes or better, while today those routes are every 20 minutes or better.

171
00:18:27,218 --> 00:18:43,130
So even for routes where we don't have any route structure changes proposed, like the 57, we are suggesting that the late night service, for example, would improve from every 15 minutes to every, sorry, from every 20 minutes to every 15 minutes instead.

172
00:18:44,222 --> 00:18:51,950
Similarly, the T-66 is largely unchanged, with the exception of a very small reroute.

173
00:18:52,551 --> 00:18:59,618
Instead of from Brookline Village traveling directly to Brigham Circle, we're proposing that it would turn up Brookline Ave.

174
00:18:59,998 --> 00:19:06,124
and cut through the Longwood medical area to get a little bit better distribution into the Longwood medical area too.

175
00:19:07,125 --> 00:19:14,752
There's many places that folks are going for employment trips and also to opportunities for patient care.

176
00:19:15,512 --> 00:19:23,317
Though I'll note that any of the routings in the immediate vicinity of the Longwood medical area are subject to be updated.

177
00:19:23,357 --> 00:19:43,490
We have a deep dive study that we are keeping up with our MassDOT team to kind of look more closely at opportunities for bus priority, where we can put in bus layovers and other infrastructure improvements in a very busy and congested area.

178
00:19:45,341 --> 00:19:47,662
We also have some other new additions.

179
00:19:48,522 --> 00:19:58,844
For example, you can see there's a route called the T15 that starts at Oak Square, goes to Brighton Center, and continues down Washington Street straight through Brookline.

180
00:19:59,644 --> 00:20:09,506
That is promoting what's today's number 65 bus, and actually extending that a little bit farther, because today the 65 starts in Brighton Center.

181
00:20:10,006 --> 00:20:13,347
So this would become a seven-day, every 15 minute or better, service.

182
00:20:13,967 --> 00:20:20,390
It's very much used by folks in Brookline to connect to the Longwood medical area for commute trips.

183
00:20:20,710 --> 00:20:28,414
Today it doesn't have Sunday service so this is actually a big increase in seven day service with new Sunday service available as well.

184
00:20:28,814 --> 00:20:31,035
And the reason it's called the T15 is because it's

185
00:20:32,536 --> 00:20:44,459
mashed up with the number 15 bus that continues from Ruggles Station over to Nubian Square across Dudley Street and eventually would continue all the way to Fields Corner.

186
00:20:44,820 --> 00:20:53,342
So this becomes a sort of crosstown linking together Alston and Brighton, Brookline, Longwood Medical Area and parts of Roxbury as well.

187
00:20:57,002 --> 00:21:01,746
There's some other changes besides those high-frequency changes with the thick blue lines.

188
00:21:02,226 --> 00:21:06,009
You can see the darker brown lines or the lighter brown lines.

189
00:21:06,049 --> 00:21:09,232
Those are our medium and our low-frequency use.

190
00:21:09,352 --> 00:21:22,322
Some key things to note, we have the number 86 bus, which you can see starts at Reservoir, sort of provides a crosstown connection up through Austin and connects over to Harvard.

191
00:21:22,622 --> 00:21:24,183
It will actually terminate at Harvard.

192
00:21:24,223 --> 00:21:25,725
Today, it continues past Harvard.

193
00:21:26,385 --> 00:21:31,231
But the other half of the 86 is being merged with another route.

194
00:21:31,371 --> 00:21:42,704
So the 86, as we know, would be truncated to only operate between Reservoir and Harvard, with a transfer available for anyone continuing on to say Union Square in Somerville or Sullivan.

195
00:21:44,255 --> 00:21:47,198
There's also an extension proposed to the Route 60.

196
00:21:47,778 --> 00:21:49,880
Today it terminates at the mall at Chestnut Hill.

197
00:21:50,340 --> 00:22:02,851
We're proposing that that would be extended a little bit farther into Newton to Newton Center to get a little extra distribution to Langley Road and some other locations there.

198
00:22:04,696 --> 00:22:13,461
And lastly here, the Route 51 does not have any specific routing changes, but it would gain new Sunday service under our proposal.

199
00:22:13,822 --> 00:22:16,503
One of our themes is to just make more consistent service.

200
00:22:17,084 --> 00:22:23,888
And in many cases, we had routes that maybe didn't operate on Sundays or their service would dramatically drop off.

201
00:22:23,928 --> 00:22:29,071
So we're proposing to keep the 51 the same, but it would gain new Sunday service.

202
00:22:30,132 --> 00:22:30,792
Next slide, please.

203
00:22:33,494 --> 00:22:40,919
This shows South Brookline and then other parts of Boston that I won't be talking about here.

204
00:22:41,520 --> 00:22:46,943
But you can see the continuation of the 51 into South Brookline.

205
00:22:47,023 --> 00:22:50,546
And as I mentioned, that area will be getting a new Sunday service.

206
00:22:50,566 --> 00:22:55,589
But overall, Brookline is getting a substantial amount of new service.

207
00:22:56,850 --> 00:23:01,313
Overall, this plan represents a 25% increase in the amount of service that we're providing.

208
00:23:02,133 --> 00:23:10,602
And whether that is on evening service or weekend service or any of these kind of consolidations to create much more high frequency service.

209
00:23:11,283 --> 00:23:15,387
And that's something that we're really very excited about providing in Brooklyn.

210
00:23:15,888 --> 00:23:16,488
Next slide, please.

211
00:23:19,572 --> 00:23:23,456
Cambridge also has a lot of high frequency service.

212
00:23:23,476 --> 00:23:24,437
So it already today.

213
00:23:25,113 --> 00:23:27,034
had a lot of high frequency service.

214
00:23:27,615 --> 00:23:30,036
Today there's five key bus routes.

215
00:23:30,737 --> 00:23:36,361
Those would be the 1, the 71, the 73, the 66, and the 77.

216
00:23:36,841 --> 00:23:45,927
And aside from that 66 change over by the Longwood Medical Area, those routes are largely the same with the better off-peak frequency because we're promoting

217
00:23:46,387 --> 00:24:00,732
As a category, all of our key bus routes to be every 15 minutes or better, rather than every 20 so that means usually late at night, or maybe on weekends that we are proposing a frequency improvement for those routes, but we're actually proposing five.

218
00:24:02,132 --> 00:24:11,215
new routes that would come into Cambridge as well, and you can see there's quite a lot of those thick blue lines if you look at the map.

219
00:24:11,555 --> 00:24:18,278
So going through, we have one connection from the south that we're calling the T39,

220
00:24:19,958 --> 00:24:28,322
This would be an extension of today's 39 bus, which starts at Forest Hills and travels through Jamaica Plain.

221
00:24:29,442 --> 00:24:41,367
We're proposing that where the 39 gets to Brigham Circle, it would, instead of paralleling the E line all the way into Copley, we propose that it would diverge from the E line.

222
00:24:41,667 --> 00:24:48,390
It would offer a transfer to the E line at Brigham Circle for anyone wishing to continue to the Back Bay or Copley areas.

223
00:24:49,162 --> 00:24:56,947
But then it would travel through the Longwood Medical Area and sort of, I'll call it a mashup with today's 47 if you're familiar with that.

224
00:24:57,047 --> 00:25:04,592
So it would connect over by the BU Bridge, connect over by BU campus through Cambridge Port to Central Square.

225
00:25:04,973 --> 00:25:12,998
And then actually we would continue it beyond Central Square to Union Square in Somerville and up to Porter Square in Cambridge.

226
00:25:14,383 --> 00:25:20,444
So this is probably the biggest departure from an existing very heavily used route.

227
00:25:20,804 --> 00:25:31,146
But one of the things that we've been trying to do is figure out how to use our bus resources most effectively and to compliment our rapid transit networks rather than duplicate the rapid transit network.

228
00:25:31,786 --> 00:25:42,448
So what this does, although it does require a transfer for some folks who are traveling from JP and other areas into the Back Bay and Copley area,

229
00:25:42,848 --> 00:25:51,171
At the same time, it's making a far more direct trip for folks from JP who might be going to Cambridge or Somerville or to Red Line locations.

230
00:25:51,591 --> 00:26:05,275
And then similarly, it provides a direct connection for anyone coming from Somerville Ave, parts of Somerville and Cambridge, traveling into the Longwood Medical Area toward BU.

231
00:26:05,715 --> 00:26:11,537
So we see that there's a lot of value, and especially with the barrier posed by the Charles River,

232
00:26:11,938 --> 00:26:24,278
This provides another opportunity to add a sort of crosstown connection to our existing crosstown connections that we already have with the Mass Ave Bridge, the One Bus, and other Charles River crossings.

233
00:26:26,178 --> 00:26:32,745
Another group that we're proposing for that would be new for Cambridge would be the T70 bus.

234
00:26:33,726 --> 00:26:42,054
This is modeled off of today's route 70, though it simplifies it a bit so it would be

235
00:26:43,351 --> 00:26:50,715
all day high frequency with every 15 minutes or better from Waltham Centre into Kendal.

236
00:26:50,815 --> 00:27:00,381
So today, sometimes the bus goes to Cedarwood and sometimes the bus goes to Marketplace Drive in Waltham and sometimes it goes to Waltham Centre.

237
00:27:00,441 --> 00:27:07,905
So we're proposing that the high frequency portion of the route would be to Waltham Centre, which means that we would actually

238
00:27:08,405 --> 00:27:18,036
drop the portion of the route in Cedarwood and restructure the portion of the route out to the Market Basket in Waltham.

239
00:27:18,557 --> 00:27:23,703
At the same time, we're also proposing to extend the route to Kendall Square in Cambridge.

240
00:27:24,103 --> 00:27:25,585
One of the things that we've been trying to do

241
00:27:26,545 --> 00:27:33,987
kind of thematically with this plan is to connect some parts of our network that just for historic reasons hadn't been well connected.

242
00:27:34,647 --> 00:27:40,429
For example, the Longwood Medical Area, Kendall Square, and South Boston Waterfront.

243
00:27:40,489 --> 00:27:48,751
You know, some of these are very large regional employment destinations, and we see that there were, you know, lots of

244
00:27:49,360 --> 00:27:51,501
folks traveling to these areas from all over the network.

245
00:27:52,142 --> 00:27:55,344
So pulling in a connection to Kendall direct from the West.

246
00:27:55,804 --> 00:28:01,848
And while we recognize this does overlap with the red line, but we saw that there was a lot of trip making happening in that quarter.

247
00:28:01,908 --> 00:28:05,010
And also it happens to be the peak load point of the red line.

248
00:28:05,610 --> 00:28:10,413
So that seemed a reasonable place to have that overlap.

249
00:28:13,475 --> 00:28:16,017
Also, we have the T96 bus.

250
00:28:16,437 --> 00:28:17,017
This becomes,

251
00:28:19,072 --> 00:28:28,159
a very exciting crosstown connection linking together the red line, the green line, and the orange line to the north of Boston.

252
00:28:29,019 --> 00:28:38,266
We're proposing to restructure today's 96, make it a little bit more direct in the sort of area near Tufts so that it can pass

253
00:28:38,756 --> 00:28:42,678
by on College Avenue directly in front of the future Medford-Tufts station.

254
00:28:43,419 --> 00:29:04,550
So it would start at Porter Square, travel up to Davis, like where you can see the existing 96 operates today, and it would travel up through Ball Square, past Medford-Tufts, and actually, unlike today's 96, which starts and ends in Medford Square, it would continue all the way up to Malden Center, sort of a mashup

255
00:29:04,932 --> 00:29:07,114
with the top half of the number 101 bus.

256
00:29:07,934 --> 00:29:17,722
And the entirety of that quarter would be promoted to all day high frequency to create a really strong sort of northern crosstown connection.

257
00:29:18,342 --> 00:29:28,790
If you recall seeing the map earlier of the existing key bus routes, you can see that it was very slim in terms of the numbers of routes

258
00:29:29,130 --> 00:29:30,531
kind of to the north of Boston.

259
00:29:30,591 --> 00:29:36,734
So this is an exciting addition to the number of high-frequency services that we have.

260
00:29:38,194 --> 00:29:42,976
We also have the T101 bus.

261
00:29:43,737 --> 00:29:55,062
On this map you can see it starts at Kendall MIT and this would connect over to Lechmere, which would be the first time that we have a sort of direct MBTA operated connection.

262
00:29:55,102 --> 00:29:55,462
I know the

263
00:29:55,762 --> 00:30:19,995
the EZ-Ride operates there and then this would continue past New Lechmere Station over the Gilmore Bridge into Charlestown and actually would serve area along Main Street in Charlestown and then continue past Sullivan up into Somerville and Medford and we have some maps that show that so I'll speak about that a little

264
00:30:20,415 --> 00:30:21,976
bit more on the next slide.

265
00:30:22,197 --> 00:30:28,822
And then lastly, the T109 bus is the fifth high frequency connection.

266
00:30:28,902 --> 00:30:41,711
So this is the route that you get when you combine the top half of the number 86 bus that I mentioned was sort of separated from the rest of the 86 with the 109 bus.

267
00:30:42,372 --> 00:30:48,517
The 109 is slated to be, that's coming from Everett, that's up and down Broadway from Linden Square.

268
00:30:49,397 --> 00:30:53,219
down through Everett and currently terminates at Sullivan.

269
00:30:53,839 --> 00:31:05,325
We proposed that that would get mashed up with the top half of the 86 to create an all day high frequency connection from Everett through Sullivan and continuing on to Union Square

270
00:31:05,966 --> 00:31:10,071
in Somerville and on to Harvard in Cambridge.

271
00:31:10,131 --> 00:31:22,907
So this is exciting because today, you know, everyone coming from Everett is sort of forced to transfer to the Orange Line, whereas this is trying to knit together suburbs that are very close to each other.

272
00:31:23,608 --> 00:31:29,891
and allow for more opportunities for direct connections and through trips.

273
00:31:30,931 --> 00:31:45,818
And also just opportunities for better frequency because today the 86, you know, sometimes on Sundays it can be every 30 or 40 minutes, but there's really quite a bit of trip-making happening in that sort of Harvard and Union Square to Sullivan Square corridor.

274
00:31:47,840 --> 00:31:53,665
So overall, you can see there's a lot more high frequency service coming to Cambridge.

275
00:31:53,685 --> 00:31:58,510
There's also some changes to the moderate frequency routes as well.

276
00:31:59,490 --> 00:32:03,474
There are a few changes that I'll point out.

277
00:32:03,594 --> 00:32:09,339
So at Kendall, you can see that we have a proposed extension of the Route 55.

278
00:32:09,419 --> 00:32:11,761
This is sort of a combination of two routes.

279
00:32:12,182 --> 00:32:13,383
It's taking today's 55, which is

280
00:32:14,925 --> 00:32:20,176
route that connects the West Fenway neighborhood in Boston to the Green Line.

281
00:32:20,236 --> 00:32:25,207
We're proposing that would continue to do so at a future accessible Heinz

282
00:32:26,009 --> 00:32:31,973
station, but we're proposing that this would then cross over the Mass Ave Bridge and continue to Kendall-MIT.

283
00:32:32,533 --> 00:32:55,168
This is sort of kind of filling in and taking the part of the role of the CT2 bus as a way to connect Red Line riders to the Longwood Medical Area, or at least some Red Line riders who might not be within walking distance of, say, the new T39 bus that's also proposed to connect other parts over to the Longwood Medical Area.

284
00:32:56,572 --> 00:33:03,395
There's also a change to the 64 bus, it would be made more consistent.

285
00:33:03,855 --> 00:33:13,799
Today, sometimes it goes to central University Park area and sometimes it goes to Kendall, we would make that change consistent seven days a week.

286
00:33:15,740 --> 00:33:17,221
and would always go to Kendall.

287
00:33:17,261 --> 00:33:33,971
There's also a proposed change that's contingent on once the future west station is completed in Austin, the 64 would be slated to use that new station, but we have some alternate routes that would be in place until that future station is completed.

288
00:33:35,151 --> 00:33:37,373
There's also a proposed route change to the 83

289
00:33:38,925 --> 00:33:48,274
Today, that is connecting from Russell Field down Rindge Avenue and connects over eventually to Central Square.

290
00:33:49,375 --> 00:34:02,167
That is proposed to instead be extended to Kendall-MIT to provide more opportunities to get from sort of the Port neighborhood and Inman Square over to Kendall because

291
00:34:03,668 --> 00:34:17,340
Again, for historic reasons, many, many routes have long terminated Central Square, but historically there hadn't been as much development as there is today in Kendall, so we're trying to reorient some of those services over to the Kendall area.

292
00:34:18,821 --> 00:34:25,287
And I'll flag that the 68 route is proposed to become a peak only service.

293
00:34:25,787 --> 00:34:28,008
So I do want to flag by peak only.

294
00:34:28,408 --> 00:34:36,812
This would include the school peak, because I recognize that those trips over to Cambridge and Luton are some of the heaviest trips that we see on that route.

295
00:34:36,872 --> 00:34:43,855
So when we define peak service, we define that to be inclusive of school travel periods.

296
00:34:45,321 --> 00:34:50,842
And then I'll flag that there are some changes for routes that you don't see here.

297
00:34:51,242 --> 00:34:53,143
But I do want to just kind of call out.

298
00:34:53,823 --> 00:34:56,104
So there's no CT2 shown on this map.

299
00:34:56,984 --> 00:34:57,844
No 85.

300
00:34:58,844 --> 00:35:02,125
The 47, as we discussed, was combined with the T39.

301
00:35:02,365 --> 00:35:03,005
So there's no 47.

302
00:35:03,705 --> 00:35:04,706
And there's no 74.

303
00:35:04,866 --> 00:35:13,828
Some of the 74 resources are slated to be sort of added to improve some of the evening span of the 75.

304
00:35:15,159 --> 00:35:23,426
and some of the off-peak frequency of the 75, but we're proposing to sort of simplify some of those West Cambridge services.

305
00:35:23,466 --> 00:35:25,107
So I did want to call that up.

306
00:35:26,788 --> 00:35:32,513
But overall, for Cambridge, there's a lot more service overall, and there's a lot more high frequency service.

307
00:35:33,371 --> 00:35:37,394
And that's something that we think can serve a lot more folks overall.

308
00:35:38,295 --> 00:35:50,603
With especially better, faster service to Longwood Medical Area, you can see we have 13,000 more residents who would have a competitive and fast trip to the LMA.

309
00:35:51,624 --> 00:35:52,144
Next slide, please.

310
00:35:57,000 --> 00:35:59,623
So Medford is an exciting area.

311
00:35:59,823 --> 00:36:08,331
Today, there are no high-frequency bus services in Medford, and we're proposing that there would be three new high-frequency routes.

312
00:36:08,351 --> 00:36:15,499
There's several municipalities, Medford, and Somerville, and Lynn, and many others that aren't

313
00:36:19,361 --> 00:36:23,865
on the high-frequency map at all today and newly get added to the high-frequency map.

314
00:36:24,385 --> 00:36:27,387
So the three connections that I'll call out in Medford.

315
00:36:28,148 --> 00:36:31,370
I'd mentioned the T96 when we were talking about Cambridge.

316
00:36:31,911 --> 00:36:35,634
You can see the bulk of the route on this map here.

317
00:36:36,494 --> 00:36:41,778
So it would connect to the new Medford Tufts station that's slated to open later this year.

318
00:36:42,299 --> 00:36:46,122
It would travel through Medford Square where today the 96 terminates and then

319
00:36:46,522 --> 00:36:59,047
It would continue along Salem Street out to Malden Center, and this is taking the place of the top half of the 101 bus that currently operates there, but also promotes it to all-day high-frequency service.

320
00:37:00,003 --> 00:37:05,807
So again, 15 minutes or better, seven days a week, 20 hours a day.

321
00:37:06,688 --> 00:37:10,331
Next, you can see there's the T101 bus.

322
00:37:11,211 --> 00:37:14,293
So this is kind of taking the other half of the 101.

323
00:37:16,455 --> 00:37:21,939
So this goes from Medford Square, would travel down Main Street.

324
00:37:22,680 --> 00:37:28,444
We actually have a reroute to keep it going all the way to Magoon Square instead of kind of

325
00:37:29,004 --> 00:37:34,687
cutting across the top of Winter Hill, so it would go down a little bit.

326
00:37:34,707 --> 00:37:52,558
I think that's Medford Street to get to Broadway, so it would travel through Magoon Square and then down Broadway and continue eventually into Kendall Square via Sullivan, via New Beechmere Station and Charlestown on their way to Kendall.

327
00:37:53,822 --> 00:38:09,730
And then the third high-frequency route that is slated for Medford, it's just a tiny corner of Medford, but provides a very useful connection, would be the T110, which connects over from Wonderland Station.

328
00:38:09,910 --> 00:38:20,955
And it's the identical routing to today's number 110 bus, which goes from Wellington across through Everett and over to Revere, where it terminates at the Blue Line.

329
00:38:21,515 --> 00:38:22,856
But it would be promoting it from

330
00:38:23,519 --> 00:38:32,366
you know, sometimes it's every 30 minutes or sometimes even longer during off-peak times, and would promote that to be every 15 minutes or better.

331
00:38:32,447 --> 00:38:36,510
So those are the three new all-day high-frequency services.

332
00:38:36,650 --> 00:38:43,136
And also, excited to point out that we're gonna have a green line in Medford soon.

333
00:38:43,316 --> 00:38:51,763
So you can see the other station in Medford there, right on the Summerville line, slated for Ball Square, coming shortly as well.

334
00:38:53,636 --> 00:38:58,019
The big thing I'll note by its absence would be the express buses.

335
00:38:58,099 --> 00:39:06,886
I know we have the 325 and 326 buses that had been suspended during the forging ahead process during COVID.

336
00:39:06,946 --> 00:39:09,308
Those changes are slated to be made permanent.

337
00:39:10,028 --> 00:39:29,968
And in general, one of the things that we've been trying to do with this map is to focus more on those seven-day-a-week travelers, trying to have all-day services, having simpler and more legible services, so things like a bus route that does the same thing seven days a week instead of sometimes

338
00:39:30,548 --> 00:39:33,489
doing one thing and then on other days doing a different thing.

339
00:39:34,270 --> 00:39:49,697
So one of the trade-offs that we've made with this map is to favor more of these all-day type connections, connecting to the future Medford Tufts station rather than having those direct express buses into Boston.

340
00:39:50,037 --> 00:39:57,981
And that was actually part of our plan even before COVID happened, but I think the pandemic has only underscored

341
00:39:59,520 --> 00:40:10,472
How important those off peak travels are because that's where we've seen some of our most durable ridership and our demand for travel into the downtown has dropped very dramatically.

342
00:40:11,313 --> 00:40:15,737
And even it's it's it's creeping back up, but some of the you know.

343
00:40:16,478 --> 00:40:18,839
hybrid schedules we think are here to stay.

344
00:40:19,559 --> 00:40:21,860
We're still continuing to monitor ridership trends.

345
00:40:22,521 --> 00:40:35,366
So as we think about the future of kind of commuting into downtowns and how much we orient our services toward the commute trips, that's something that we continue to monitor.

346
00:40:37,063 --> 00:40:42,487
Now there are other, besides the high frequency changes, we do have some other changes.

347
00:40:42,727 --> 00:40:49,591
So if you look at the dark brown roof that provides that sort of crosstown connection, that's the proposed new route 95.

348
00:40:50,132 --> 00:40:52,914
Today it actually goes to Sullivan.

349
00:40:52,954 --> 00:40:56,916
There's the bus route 95 that goes via Mystic Ave down to Sullivan.

350
00:40:56,936 --> 00:41:02,720
So we've flipped this to be more of a crosstown within Medford over to Wellington Station instead.

351
00:41:03,361 --> 00:41:04,762
This would go down Riverside Ave.

352
00:41:05,582 --> 00:41:09,206
And we're proposing that it would cut down to Mystic Valley Parkway.

353
00:41:09,767 --> 00:41:14,172
As we understand, there's an amount of new developments slated for that corridor.

354
00:41:14,612 --> 00:41:24,103
And also it provides connectivity to the retail Wegmans and other locations where it cuts down from Riverside Ave.

355
00:41:25,914 --> 00:41:33,536
At the outer end, on the West Medford end, this would be a route that would be extended consistently to Arlington Center.

356
00:41:33,977 --> 00:41:43,179
Today, half the trips go to Arlington Center and half the trips go to Playstead Road, where it intersects with Winthrop Street.

357
00:41:44,520 --> 00:41:51,042
So we're proposing, again, simplifying this to make one route that does one thing consistently.

358
00:41:52,002 --> 00:42:05,850
And that also means that there would be more frequent connections to Arlington Center and it becomes sort of a cross Medford route.

359
00:42:05,910 --> 00:42:08,772
This would be every 30 minutes or better, seven days a week.

360
00:42:10,470 --> 00:42:13,353
some other changes slated for Medford.

361
00:42:13,493 --> 00:42:19,217
We have the 94, which is doing a number of different things.

362
00:42:20,278 --> 00:42:28,505
So today, the 94 does connect from Davis to the West Medford area, where it views sort of across the Medford Square.

363
00:42:29,185 --> 00:42:33,629
But that was sort of, today's 94 is sort of, I'll call it V-shaped.

364
00:42:34,149 --> 00:42:38,592
And we've been trying to make routes that are more straight, so that they can be

365
00:42:40,025 --> 00:42:42,090
just generally more competitive with driving.

366
00:42:42,150 --> 00:42:45,437
You know, if someone were starting at Medford Square trying to get to the red line.

367
00:42:45,962 --> 00:42:50,544
They generally would never have driven there, biked there via West Medford.

368
00:42:50,604 --> 00:42:57,666
So we said, well, what if we structure this in a different way to get folks from Boston Ave and West Medford?

369
00:42:58,267 --> 00:43:00,347
We send the bus out Placestead Road.

370
00:43:00,587 --> 00:43:07,550
So this fills in from the portion of Placestead Road that was formerly covered by half of the 95 trips.

371
00:43:08,010 --> 00:43:14,413
And then we continue the bus up, actually out to Winchester, covering parts of the route that were formerly covered by the 134 bus.

372
00:43:16,202 --> 00:43:18,445
which would no longer operate in this.

373
00:43:18,606 --> 00:43:31,182
And it actually then continues out to the Burlington Mall and to the Third Ave area, Burlington, like where the Wegmans and other restaurants and other things are located.

374
00:43:32,496 --> 00:43:41,102
So this becomes sort of, this is just, we call it the 94, but this is quite a bit of a departure from today's 94 bus.

375
00:43:41,763 --> 00:44:00,936
And it would still provide a connection to the red line at Davis and perhaps also provides those reverse commute opportunities for folks going from Medford or actually out to Winchester and Burlington and some of the other suburbs as well.

376
00:44:03,779 --> 00:44:11,981
