Yeah, so we will be collecting all the feedback that we're getting from these meetings, online surveys, all the other ways that I mentioned earlier that we're collecting feedback, and they'll be going into like one database where we'll use that to make decisions about how we're going to inform the final map.

986
01:45:57,983 --> 01:46:05,547
We are right now thinking about moving towards taking that final map and bring it directly to the MBTA board in the fall.

987
01:46:06,167 --> 01:46:11,430
And then we will be doing a public process afterwards just to communicate what's included in the final map.

988
01:46:12,110 --> 01:46:14,571
That's the way that we're thinking about that process right now.

989
01:46:15,732 --> 01:46:15,952
Great.

990
01:46:16,292 --> 01:46:16,632
Thank you.

991
01:46:18,914 --> 01:46:20,534
Emily Bargava.

992
01:46:25,357 --> 01:46:25,597
Emily?

993
01:46:26,907 --> 01:46:27,848
You can unmute yourself.

994
01:46:29,049 --> 01:46:30,430
Yeah, that wasn't my last name.

995
01:46:30,590 --> 01:46:32,491
Sorry, I didn't know you were talking.

996
01:46:32,671 --> 01:46:34,052
Oh, I'm sorry.

997
01:46:34,713 --> 01:46:35,774
It's okay.

998
01:46:36,014 --> 01:46:42,119
So my question is about the proposed change from the 95 on Mystic to the 87.

999
01:46:44,689 --> 01:46:51,411
We have a bus lane on Mystic Ave that has never actually been implemented correctly.

1000
01:46:52,491 --> 01:47:01,814
And it's really disheartening to hear that we'll be getting a change from one bus to one other bus and there won't be more frequent service.

1001
01:47:03,115 --> 01:47:04,495
What's the point of the bus lane?

1002
01:47:04,515 --> 01:47:08,536
I mean, I know the point of the bus lane because it doesn't actually exist.

1003
01:47:08,616 --> 01:47:10,957
It's three hours, there's no enforcement.

1004
01:47:11,646 --> 01:47:13,027
So there it is.

1005
01:47:13,727 --> 01:47:25,135
But currently, if I want to go westbound, I have to walk eight minutes to Shore Drive or eight minutes to where the stop is in front of the Burger King on Mystic.

1006
01:47:25,815 --> 01:47:34,541
And I have to cross Mystic at some of the most dangerous crossings in Medford and Somerville, where people are regularly hit by cars.

1007
01:47:36,002 --> 01:47:45,250
So I appreciate that the 87 will go to Davis now, but it doesn't make any sense for me to risk my life crossing the street to go to Davis.

1008
01:47:45,650 --> 01:47:51,875
But of course, now I also could walk up the hill to Broadway and take the 89, except, oh wait, the 89 is gone.

1009
01:47:53,516 --> 01:48:02,499
So I feel like mid-Somerville, Winter Hill is getting the short shift of this bus redesign.

1010
01:48:03,599 --> 01:48:06,000
Because from Winter Hill, we can't get to Davis Square anymore.

1011
01:48:06,620 --> 01:48:12,622
And if we want to take the Green Line, we have to pay more for it, because the train costs more than the bus.

1012
01:48:14,383 --> 01:48:17,164
So that's it.

1013
01:48:17,184 --> 01:48:20,085
89 Mystic Ave bus lane doesn't really work.

1014
01:48:20,985 --> 01:48:22,566
And what are you going to do to fix that?

1015
01:48:22,846 --> 01:48:23,166
Thank you.

1016
01:48:24,391 --> 01:48:24,792
Thank you.

1017
01:48:25,032 --> 01:48:26,916
Melissa, do you want to respond?

1018
01:48:30,281 --> 01:48:34,549
Melissa, I can respond about the bustling part, but if you want to talk about anything else, feel free.

1019
01:48:35,270 --> 01:48:35,751
OK, no.

1020
01:48:36,232 --> 01:48:36,893
Go ahead, Andrew.

1021
01:48:38,462 --> 01:48:40,842
Yeah, so thanks for your comments.

1022
01:48:41,303 --> 01:48:46,704
For the bus lane design, totally hear the concerns.

1023
01:48:47,604 --> 01:48:55,605
It's definitely something that we, whenever we do designs and work with roadway owners, we're trying to do those processes often iteratively.

1024
01:48:55,665 --> 01:49:07,988
So I think some of those comments you brought up are all the more reason we might want to just kind of follow up with some of the roadway owners and just kind of talk through and see other ways that we can figure out ways to improve the design.

1025
01:49:09,809 --> 01:49:11,190
But thank you for that comment.

1026
01:49:12,091 --> 01:49:14,232
I did just want to do a time check.

1027
01:49:15,093 --> 01:49:20,597
We have just enough staff time to kind of go until 8pm.

1028
01:49:21,758 --> 01:49:26,922
So we'll take a few more questions, but just wanted to say that we will have to end the meeting at 8pm.

1029
01:49:29,265 --> 01:49:33,128
Jason, do you want to do the next person?

1030
01:49:33,549 --> 01:49:33,769
Thanks.

1031
01:49:33,849 --> 01:49:34,089
Sure.

1032
01:49:34,670 --> 01:49:36,211
I'm going to read a comment.

1033
01:49:36,371 --> 01:49:46,200
The lower frequency and route change of bus 9496 makes it harder to travel between Medford Hillside and Davis Harvard Medford Square.

1034
01:49:50,663 --> 01:49:54,607
Someone want to try to respond or answer that concern?

1035
01:50:00,747 --> 01:50:04,790
Certainly just in general, this is our draft plan.

1036
01:50:04,850 --> 01:50:09,252
So we're trying to hear from folks which connections work better or not.

1037
01:50:09,353 --> 01:50:23,382
Though I'll flag that T96 is a big frequency boost from today's 96 levels, though 94 is largely the same in the forecasted map, in the proposed map.

1038
01:50:24,682 --> 01:50:25,583
Okay, thank you.

1039
01:50:28,125 --> 01:50:30,046
Let's go to Daryl Haynes.

1040
01:50:34,693 --> 01:50:35,374
Can you hear me?

1041
01:50:37,435 --> 01:50:37,996
Okay, thanks.

1042
01:50:38,276 --> 01:50:42,099
I'm going to expand on a comment I made in the breakout session about mainly focusing on the 83.

1043
01:50:42,520 --> 01:50:46,043
But I first want to say, I think you're missing an opportunity with the 68.

1044
01:50:47,404 --> 01:50:51,647
And you're reducing the service there when in fact, I think there's an opportunity to expand it.

1045
01:50:52,088 --> 01:50:59,134
If you understand that the library main library, which is very well used closes many nights at nine o'clock.

1046
01:50:59,794 --> 01:51:02,697
And the 68 is right there by the library.

1047
01:51:02,717 --> 01:51:02,937
And so

1048
01:51:03,438 --> 01:51:09,920
If you don't have numbers for stuff that isn't currently happening, it's hard for you to understand how it could be beneficial.

1049
01:51:11,181 --> 01:51:18,664
But on the 83, I think you're making a mistake in commission and omission.

1050
01:51:18,704 --> 01:51:28,888
The commission is, I concur with what other people have said, you want people to take the 83 to Porter and then go down three long escalators to wait for a red line that sometimes doesn't come.

1051
01:51:30,401 --> 01:51:40,085
I think this is just a horrible imposition for low income people, the heaviest concentration of which are right where I'm standing out where the 83 starts at Como Field.

1052
01:51:40,585 --> 01:51:47,548
So I think really you ought to take a very careful look at not continuing a service of the 83 to Central.

1053
01:51:47,848 --> 01:51:51,149
And there's an opportunity to extend it in the other direction or maybe add

1054
01:51:51,670 --> 01:52:03,674
some other kind of service so that all the low-income people who are crammed in here could actually get over the bridge and get to the Fresh Pond Mall, at least, where the buses sometimes already have to go when the parking lot by Como Field is full.

1055
01:52:03,934 --> 01:52:07,075
They already go down to Fresh Pond Mall and make a swing around that rotary.

1056
01:52:07,435 --> 01:52:12,436
Why not extend that at least part of the day so people can go do their shopping at the mall?

1057
01:52:12,896 --> 01:52:14,437
And I would say extend it even further

1058
01:52:14,917 --> 01:52:21,664
down to Aberdeen or something like that so that people connect to Watertown without having to go all the way into Harvard Square.

1059
01:52:21,704 --> 01:52:23,366
And I agree with the 96, too.

1060
01:52:23,386 --> 01:52:26,049
The 96 into Harvard Square and from Harvard Square is quite useful.

1061
01:52:26,730 --> 01:52:27,210
Anyway, thank you.

1062
01:52:27,230 --> 01:52:29,533
Thank you, Daryl.

1063
01:52:33,878 --> 01:52:35,940
Who wants to take this one, Melissa or Andrew?

1064
01:52:40,373 --> 01:52:41,674
Thanks, James.

1065
01:52:41,954 --> 01:52:49,800
We've talked about this at the Cambridge Transit Advisory Committee or some other forum.

1066
01:52:49,840 --> 01:52:51,742
So thanks for the feedback.

1067
01:52:52,362 --> 01:52:58,867
And this is the type of feedback that we'll consider as we get ready to make the next version of the map.

1068
01:53:01,769 --> 01:53:02,109
Thank you.

1069
01:53:03,170 --> 01:53:04,991
So there were two Emilies with their hands up.

1070
01:53:05,051 --> 01:53:09,615
I tried to call on Emily Bargava earlier, so I'll give it one more try.

1071
01:53:09,715 --> 01:53:09,955
Emily?

1072
01:53:13,842 --> 01:53:14,382
Hello, thank you.

1073
01:53:14,442 --> 01:53:17,604
This is actually Rahul Bhargav, but thanks for bringing me back.

1074
01:53:18,704 --> 01:53:33,791
I am a Somerville resident, and I just wanted to reinforce some of the comments I've heard already, particularly around the idea that the crosstown service from either, if you want to call it east to west or north to south, it's just a real gap here.

1075
01:53:34,811 --> 01:53:40,654
And it's doing a real disservice to the Green Line extension to not connect it to any of the other bus lines

1076
01:53:40,854 --> 01:53:53,006
red line is multiple connected to bus lines, the orange line is multiple connections to bus lines, and really only at the Medford Tufts station is there any connection here all the way down to the green line.

1077
01:53:53,226 --> 01:53:56,929
So I would really strongly advocate for keeping a line like the 89

1078
01:53:57,930 --> 01:53:59,671
which goes from one side to the other.

1079
01:53:59,691 --> 01:54:12,281
I think that the idea that everyone's going to take the 90 and that it really isn't increasing in frequency significantly is, I think, is sort of a poor solution.

1080
01:54:12,861 --> 01:54:16,384
And the idea that everyone's going to jump on the green line is similarly a poor solution.

1081
01:54:16,404 --> 01:54:19,126
I think across the system, you have really nice redundancy.

1082
01:54:19,586 --> 01:54:21,728
And I think we need to see some echo of that.

1083
01:54:22,168 --> 01:54:25,770
particularly in that Winter Hill gap that another speaker mentioned.

1084
01:54:26,550 --> 01:54:36,034
And I'd love to sort of hear some of your thoughts about how those those gaps in the bus service play out between Clarendon Hill to Powder House to Winter Hill.

1085
01:54:37,455 --> 01:54:47,943
And what I've looked at the alternate approaches and again, sort of the 90 where it is, you know, there's tons of traffic and increasing its frequency isn't going to work.

1086
01:54:47,983 --> 01:54:52,446
And it doesn't even, isn't even significantly proposed here on this plan to be a 15 minute route.

1087
01:54:52,866 --> 01:55:02,273
So anyway, I just want to do a shout out to that, those gaps I see, and I feel like there's a better trade off that could be made there that I'd encourage you to reconsider, which echoes points other folks has made.

1088
01:55:02,493 --> 01:55:03,074
Thank you for the time.

1089
01:55:04,935 --> 01:55:05,796
Thank you for your comments.

1090
01:55:07,791 --> 01:55:08,031
Andrew?

1091
01:55:10,192 --> 01:55:14,715
I think those are great points and definitely echo some of the points that folks brought up earlier.

1092
01:55:14,775 --> 01:55:18,677
So we'll look into them as we're going into the draft map, but thanks for raising them.

1093
01:55:18,697 --> 01:55:23,379
I think we have time for just like one final question and then we do have to wrap up, so.

1094
01:55:24,980 --> 01:55:25,120
Yeah.

1095
01:55:25,841 --> 01:55:30,163
All right, let's go to, let's see, Michael Kirpin.

1096
01:55:35,018 --> 01:55:35,578
Hi there.

1097
01:55:35,598 --> 01:55:42,063
I was just wanting to ask about also the Somerville area.

1098
01:55:43,124 --> 01:55:47,207
What are you going to do about all the connections you broke?

1099
01:55:47,888 --> 01:55:52,291
You can't get from Sullivan to Davis anymore in your plan.

1100
01:55:52,751 --> 01:56:02,519
You can't get from Lechmere to any of the intermediate destinations that aren't served by the Green Line anymore.

1101
01:56:03,259 --> 01:56:08,462
You've completely cut people off and there's these huge gaps.

1102
01:56:08,722 --> 01:56:17,328
Other people have talked about it before, but I just wanted to reiterate, how are you going to fix the gaps in Somerville?

1103
01:56:17,348 --> 01:56:23,872
Thank you for your question.

1104
01:56:26,513 --> 01:56:28,855
Melissa or Andrew, do you want to take a stab at answering that?

1105
01:56:31,485 --> 01:56:33,366
Yeah, we're all ears.

1106
01:56:33,766 --> 01:56:38,007
And I know, as I mentioned, we have some ideas that we've been kicking around.

1107
01:56:38,227 --> 01:56:42,508
If you have any specific suggestions on send this down that way.

1108
01:56:43,488 --> 01:56:48,890
Those are the types of things that we're going to be sharpening our pencils and taking another look over the coming

1109
01:56:49,710 --> 01:56:58,597
weeks, as we get closer to the new version of the map, this sort of September timeframe.

1110
01:56:58,697 --> 01:57:03,881
So if you have kind of specific links, but the one, the one kind of

1111
01:57:07,243 --> 01:57:10,204
Complicating piece is the resources question.

1112
01:57:10,624 --> 01:57:22,668
So we have a proposal that, you know, meets our sort of 25% more bus operators target and works for the number of buses that were slated to have in the near future.

1113
01:57:22,749 --> 01:57:26,250
So as we're thinking about, you know, adding new connections and new links.

1114
01:57:26,990 --> 01:57:32,032
The question is like, how do we do we dial down frequencies in different places in order to make the resources work.

1115
01:57:32,273 --> 01:57:36,895
That's the tricky bit that is on us to figure out.

1116
01:57:36,915 --> 01:57:41,817
But, you know, if you have specific ideas on, you know, hey, have this rig here or zag there.

1117
01:57:43,378 --> 01:57:43,958
We're all ears.

1118
01:57:44,198 --> 01:57:45,819
And that's what we're trying to

1119
01:57:48,332 --> 01:57:55,155
kind of gather as many of those good ideas so that we can come up with a better version of the plan, because we probably didn't get it right.

1120
01:57:55,535 --> 01:57:58,256
So how do we fix it and how do we make something that's even better?

1121
01:57:58,636 --> 01:57:59,256
That's what we're here for.

1122
01:58:00,757 --> 01:58:01,497
Thank you, Melissa.

1123
01:58:02,556 --> 01:58:04,217
All right, well, this was exciting.

1124
01:58:04,257 --> 01:58:21,071
I'm sorry we have to end the meeting, but I'm really excited about all the comments and the interest in all those who are in attendance who are really invested in this project and offered some really thoughtful suggestions, questions, and comments.

1125
01:58:23,093 --> 01:58:24,134
It's a long process.

1126
